32
00:03:10,126 --> 00:03:14,020
We don't need any categories, priorities, due dates, or even authentication not yet.

33
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,760
We only need the main task loop working properly.

34
00:03:18,158 --> 00:03:19,940
So here's the prompt that we're gonna use here.

35
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:28,240
Build a simple task management app where users can add a task by typing in an input field and pressing enter or clicking add.

36
00:03:28,654 --> 00:03:32,516
Each task should show the task title and a delete button.

37
00:03:32,942 --> 00:03:35,012
Display all tasks in a clean list.

38
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,172
Use a minimal white background design with a blue accent color.

39
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:43,520
No categories, no priorities, no due dates, no authentication yet.

40
00:03:43,854 --> 00:03:48,560
And then once base 44 finishes the build, we can test the core feature immediately.

41
00:03:48,974 --> 00:03:54,532
So I'm gonna type in a simple task here. I'm gonna press enter and then confirm that it does appear in my list.

42
00:03:54,800 --> 00:04:04,004
I can also use the add button. I can check that the task title does display correctly, and that can confirm that the delete button also works as expected.

43
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:14,116
So the simple test here tells us more than a complicated first generation would because now we can already see whether the task data is actually being stored properly,

44
00:04:14,382 --> 00:04:19,680
whether the input and the list are connected, and whether the basic interface logic works.

45
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:26,724
