153
00:16:21,294 --> 00:16:29,104
Adding the same feature without planning could still produce something that might work today, yes, but it might also store the

154
00:16:29,104 --> 00:16:34,532
information in a way that then becomes difficult to connect to projects or teams later on.

155
00:16:34,990 --> 00:16:47,268
Discuss mode just gives us the chance to make those decisions while they are still easy to change, keeping each new feature cleaner and reducing the amount of rebuilding required as the app grows.

156
00:16:47,598 --> 00:16:54,576
Now here's the thing: an app can look completely ready and good to go, but still have a serious problem hiding underneath.

157
00:16:54,736 --> 00:17:02,852
The pages load, yes, the login works, and then every single button does what it should, so it's easy to assume the app is safe to publish.

158
00:17:03,246 --> 00:17:09,060
However, security issues are different because you usually cannot see them by clicking around the interface.

159
00:17:09,454 --> 00:17:15,012
AI code generators are mainly focused on just making the feature that you requested actually work.

160
00:17:15,216 --> 00:17:22,564
And if you ask for authentication, a database, or a new user flow, then the AI will prioritize getting that functionality running.

161
00:17:22,830 --> 00:17:31,296
It may not automatically catch every exposed API key, weak access rule, or vulnerable dependency sitting behind the finished result.

162
00:17:31,822 --> 00:17:37,924
So that is why we do need to run base 44's built-in security scan before publishing the task management app.

163
00:17:38,254 --> 00:17:39,776
Now the scan is free here.

164
00:17:39,950 --> 00:17:49,924
It takes less than a minute and it checks for common vulnerabilities that could otherwise just remain hidden until real users are already inside of the app.

165
00:17:50,318 --> 00:17:55,492
So inside base 44, we can open up the security scan here and then trigger it before the app goes hive.

166
00:17:55,854 --> 00:18:01,828
After that, base 44 will show us any issues that it finds, along with an explanation and a recommended fix.

167
00:18:02,318 --> 00:18:10,176
Now the scan may flag an exposed API key, which means sensitive credentials could be visible somewhere that they shouldn't be at all.

168
00:18:10,446 --> 00:18:17,700
It can also detect broken access control rules where one user may be able to reach data that belongs to somebody else.

169
00:18:18,318 --> 00:18:28,804
Dependency vulnerabilities are also another common warning here, which means part of the app relies on software with a known security issue.

170
00:18:29,230 --> 00:18:35,044
So we're going to go through each result here in plain language so it's clear what the warning actually means.

171
00:18:35,470 --> 00:18:44,708
And base44 also provides a recommended action for each of these issues, so we can apply the fixes one at a time rather than guessing what needs to change.

172
00:18:45,230 --> 00:18:51,108
So after these fixes are applied, we will run the scan one more time and confirm that the app passes.

173
00:18:51,470 --> 00:18:54,336
Only then is it really ready to move into the publishing stage.

174
00:18:54,606 --> 00:19:00,900
Now this should become part of every single build, even when the app is small or only has a few users.

175
00:19:01,358 --> 00:19:03,616
Running the scan takes less than a minute anyway.

176
00:19:03,886 --> 00:19:10,084
Skipping it can just leave a problem unnoticed until it's already affected real accounts and real data.

177
00:19:10,414 --> 00:19:19,716
Making it a required step before every publish is one of the simplest ways to avoid shipping an app that works on the surface great but is not secure underneath.

178
00:19:20,078 --> 00:19:24,996
One of the easiest mistakes now to make is testing an app only under perfect conditions.

179
00:19:25,422 --> 00:19:31,652
You add a couple of short tasks, you click through the main features, and everything seems rosy, appears to work.

180
00:19:32,078 --> 00:19:42,404
The problem is that real users will enter longer titles, miss those deadlines, complete dozens of tasks, and then use the app in ways that clean sample content never really prepares us for.

181
00:19:42,894 --> 00:19:47,428
Placeholder content usually represents the easiest possible version of the app.

182
00:19:47,854 --> 00:19:56,260
Task titles fit neatly on one line. Every deadline is in the future, and there are never enough completed tasks to crowd the screen.

183
00:19:56,654 --> 00:20:06,052
So those examples help, yes, during the early build, but they do not reveal the layout problems, those broken logic flows or edge cases that appear during actual use.

184
00:20:06,542 --> 00:20:13,124
So for this task management app, realistic test data means adding tasks that someone might genuinely create.

